AI Automation Roadmap
Objective
Generate maintainable BDD automation from manual QA test cases while maximizing reuse of the existing automation framework and minimizing locator maintenance.
Phase 1 - Framework Discovery
Goal
Create a catalog of all existing automation capabilities.
Inputs
Automation Framework Repository
Existing Step Definitions
Existing Business APIs
Existing Assertions
Deliverables
framework-capabilities.md
Example:
Authentication
login()
logout()
Employee Search
searchEmployee()
searchByName()
resetSearch()
Approval
approveRequest()
rejectRequest()
Success Criteria
Existing capabilities are documented.
Duplicate APIs are identified.
Reusable APIs are identified.
Phase 2 - UI Discovery
Goal
Build a knowledge base of the Angular application.
Inputs
Angular Repository
Screenshots
Existing Automation Artifacts
Deliverables
ui-knowledge-base.md
For each page:
Page Name
Business Purpose
Controls
Navigation Paths
Labels
Success Criteria
Screens are documented.
User actions are identified.
UI controls are mapped.
Phase 3 - Locator Strategy
Goal
Centralize locator management.
Deliverables
locator.yaml
locator-strategy.md
Priority:
data-testid
id
formcontrolname
name
aria-label
visible text
xpath
Never use:
Absolute XPath
Index-based XPath
ng-star classes
Auto-generated IDs
Success Criteria
No hardcoded locators in Java.
All locators managed through locator.yaml.
Phase 4 - Automation Reuse Analysis
Goal
Prevent duplicate automation generation.
Deliverables
automation-reuse-catalog.md
For each business capability:
Existing API
Existing Step Definition
Existing Assertion
Success Criteria
Reusable assets are cataloged.
Duplication opportunities identified.
Phase 5 - Manual Test Analysis
Goal
Map manual test cases to existing automation assets.
Inputs
Manual Test Cases
framework-capabilities.md
automation-reuse-catalog.md
ui-knowledge-base.md
Deliverables
reuse-plan.md
For each manual test:
Existing APIs to reuse
Existing Steps to reuse
Missing capabilities
Success Criteria
Reuse-first approach established.
Gaps identified before coding.
Phase 6 - Automation Generation
Goal
Generate automation using existing framework capabilities.
Rules
Reuse existing APIs first.
Reuse existing Step Definitions first.
Reuse existing Assertions first.
Create new APIs only if no capability exists.
Never generate direct Selenium code.
Never generate hardcoded locators.
Deliverables
Feature File
Step Definitions
New Business APIs (if required)
locator.yaml updates (if required)
Phase 7 - Self-Healing Locator Framework
Goal
Reduce maintenance when UI changes.
Approach
locator.yaml supports:
Primary locator
Fallback locator(s)
Framework attempts:
Primary → Fallback1 → Fallback2 → Fallback3
Future Enhancement
AI-assisted locator healing:
Capture DOM
Capture Screenshot
Identify replacement locator
Update locator.yaml
Success Criteria
UI changes do not require test rewrites.
Most locator failures are automatically recovered. :::
File 2: DEVIN_AUTOMATION_INSTRUCTIONS.md
Writing
Devin Automation Instructions
Context
This project uses:
BDD Feature Files → Step Definitions → Business Action APIs → Internal Automation Framework → Locator Repository → Selenium
You must follow this architecture.
Mandatory Rules
Rule 1
Do NOT generate Selenium code directly unless explicitly requested.
Avoid:
WebDriver calls
WebElement usage
@FindBy
Direct XPath usage
Rule 2
Always reuse existing framework capabilities.
Before generating code:
Read framework-capabilities.md
Read automation-reuse-catalog.md
Search for reusable APIs first.
Rule 3
Do not create duplicate business APIs.
Bad:
searchEmployeeById() performEmployeeSearch() employeeSearch()
Good:
Reuse existing searchEmployee()
Rule 4
Generate business-level automation.
Preferred:
When user searches employee by employee id
Avoid:
When user clicks Search Button When user enters Employee ID
BDD should describe business behavior, not UI actions.
Rule 5
All UI interactions must go through framework APIs.
Bad:
driver.findElement(...) click()
Good:
employeeSearchApi.searchEmployee()
Rule 6
All locators must be stored in locator.yaml.
Never hardcode:
XPath
CSS Selectors
IDs
inside Java classes.
Rule 7
When a new UI element is required:
Update locator.yaml
Use locator strategy priority
Priority:
data-testid
id
formcontrolname
name
aria-label
text
xpath
Rule 8
Before generating automation, perform a reuse analysis.
Output:
Reuse:
Existing APIs
Existing Step Definitions
Existing Assertions
Missing:
New APIs required
New Locators required
Generate code only after reuse analysis.
Rule 9
Generate the minimum amount of code necessary.
Preferred:
Reuse existing APIs.
Create new APIs only when no existing capability satisfies the requirement.
Rule 10
For every manual test case:
Perform this sequence:
Analyze manual test case.
Read framework capabilities.
Read automation reuse catalog.
Read UI knowledge base.
Produce reuse plan.
Generate automation.
Update locator.yaml if required.
Never skip the reuse analysis step.
Desired Outcome
Manual Test Case ↓ Reuse Analysis ↓ Existing Business APIs ↓ BDD Automation ↓ Minimal New Code
Goal:
Maximum Reuse Minimum Selenium Minimum Locator Maintenance Minimum Framework Duplication
If you put these two files at the root of the automation repository and force every Devin task to start by reading them, you'll get far more consistent automation generation than relying on ad-hoc prompts every time.
